WEBVTT

00:00.812 --> 00:02.993
Today is February 2nd, 2026.

00:03.073 --> 00:06.855
My name is Caitlyn Sheets and I'm currently in the Glasscock building, is that right?

00:07.136 --> 00:07.856
In the basement.

00:08.856 --> 00:15.480
I'm here with Lauren and we're conducting this interview on Texas A&M's campus and College Station.

00:15.820 --> 00:21.665
The interview is part of an oral history project documenting the experiences of Aggies for the 150th celebration of Texas A&M.

00:22.326 --> 00:23.928
Do I have your consent to record?

00:24.008 --> 00:24.168
Yes.

00:24.508 --> 00:24.808
Awesome.

00:24.828 --> 00:25.409
Thank you so much.

00:25.549 --> 00:30.874
So I'm here with Lauren, as I said, a former undergrad, currently a new grad student.

00:31.234 --> 00:31.775
That's what I thought.

00:37.233 --> 01:00.438
yes that's awesome um so thank you for joining me and sharing your story um thank you um can you give me a little bit of background like before you came to texas a&m yeah where you're born yeah so i was born in cincinnati um i come from rural ohio people and i still say pop yeah good stuff um there was a scary week there with the college playoffs

01:02.198 --> 01:07.689
would have gone to Ohio State if we beat Miami, but alas, Carson Beck made that not an issue.

01:09.278 --> 01:12.660
Yeah, so I always imagined I'd go back up to Ohio for school.

01:12.800 --> 01:14.721
All my extended family is still up there.

01:14.741 --> 01:17.482
My dad went to my university in Oxford, Ohio.

01:17.522 --> 01:20.624
My mom went to Iowa State for a semester before going to cosmetology school.

01:20.684 --> 01:23.645
So a lot of roots are in Ohio.

01:23.685 --> 01:28.908
We moved down here 20 years ago this summer, which is crazy.

01:29.028 --> 01:31.189
We went to Sugar Land, Missouri City area.

01:31.249 --> 01:33.730
I was there in elementary school until 5th grade.

01:33.830 --> 01:35.251
And then we moved to the Woodlands.

01:35.531 --> 01:37.392
I was there 5th through...

01:38.132 --> 01:39.293
You know, until I went to college.

01:39.333 --> 01:42.335
Yeah, I have two siblings.

01:42.416 --> 01:45.038
One's a twin sister who did not pursue higher ed at all.

01:45.458 --> 01:49.061
And then my brother who went to A&M who kind of fed me the Kool-Aid.

01:49.581 --> 01:50.482
Oh, is that where it came from?

01:50.502 --> 01:50.802
Yes.

01:51.343 --> 01:54.785
My sister and I visited him one weekend and I was like, this isn't so bad.

01:54.845 --> 01:55.466
Yeah, sure.

01:56.719 --> 02:03.723
And then after high school, I was encouraged slash you're going to do Lone Star Community College for a year.

02:03.823 --> 02:10.947
I only had to do it a year instead of two to get my full associate since I graduated high school with like 27 college credits.

02:10.967 --> 02:14.629
My high school is a very, very huge proponent for AP, dual credits.

02:14.649 --> 02:15.610
Which high school?

02:15.630 --> 02:16.190
Woodlands High School.

02:16.210 --> 02:16.551
Woodlands?

02:16.691 --> 02:17.451
Okay, that's awesome.

02:17.691 --> 02:18.712
Yeah, we're 6A.

02:18.832 --> 02:19.012
It's...

02:19.596 --> 02:23.400
It's hard to explain, but everything's competition and the Woodlands.

02:23.420 --> 02:24.261
That's stressful.

02:24.321 --> 02:24.821
Yes, it is.

02:24.901 --> 02:28.885
If you want to do varsity or be the lead in the, I did theater in high school.

02:28.925 --> 02:33.590
So if you wanted to do leads, I was on the improv troupe in high school.

02:33.770 --> 02:37.413
I was the president for two years and it was pretty much, you have to start doing that when you're five.

02:37.874 --> 02:38.154
Wow.

02:38.174 --> 02:39.475
Because it's a six day.

02:39.515 --> 02:40.617
I graduated with 1100 kids.

02:42.347 --> 02:44.128
So it's a different culture.

02:44.168 --> 02:45.328
Yes, very much.

02:45.368 --> 02:46.409
Yes, it's very competitive.

02:46.809 --> 02:48.789
Academics and athletics equally.

02:48.809 --> 02:52.591
I think that's kind of hard to find, but again, it's Texas.

02:52.611 --> 02:56.012
So academics have always been present in your mind.

02:56.232 --> 02:56.452
Yes.

02:56.752 --> 02:59.613
I wouldn't say, though, I tried in high school.

03:00.154 --> 03:02.835
English particularly became very, very easy to me.

03:02.895 --> 03:04.675
I was always a really big reader as a kid.

03:04.695 --> 03:07.897
I have an edition of Little House on the Prairie on my bookshelf.

03:09.752 --> 03:13.735
That is a good example of why I am the way I am today.

03:13.755 --> 03:15.436
The breadcrumbs are always, always there.

03:15.456 --> 03:22.561
I originally was actually going to study English, but they recommended to transfer into A&M.

03:22.621 --> 03:24.983
You already had your foreign language done, and I did not.

03:25.543 --> 03:33.369
And so the BS of history here at A&M offers without the foreign language, and that was the only other major I was interested in, so I kind of naturally...

03:33.818 --> 04:03.471
went in there I'm gonna say that might have been the first year they offered that so the timing was very very kismet and I'm very very thankful um and I was an English minor so it's still a big part of my life it's a big philosophy of my history training and all that but yeah yeah academics have always been a big part of me it's definitely been I was embarrassed to get these I was that kid in English who like if Lauren didn't read we're shrewd yeah math and science were always pretty hard for me I never really that's fair yeah I never really got that but

04:03.925 --> 04:07.327
Was there like an English teacher professor that really encouraged you?

04:07.407 --> 04:10.949
Yeah, I had a wonderful junior and senior.

04:10.969 --> 04:12.250
So both of those were dual credits.

04:12.611 --> 04:14.632
Dr. Heidi Bardenhagen was my junior.

04:15.152 --> 04:17.974
And then Dr. No, he's not a doctor, sorry.

04:18.014 --> 04:19.715
Jeff Murren was my senior.

04:19.775 --> 04:23.838
And like he used to be the only teacher who taught senior year dual credits.

04:23.858 --> 04:28.380
So it was like my brother had him when he taught at McCullough, the junior high school.

04:28.401 --> 04:29.761
So then he moved to the high school.

04:30.042 --> 04:30.842
He's just amazing.

04:30.882 --> 04:32.343
He's tatted up and he's just awesome.

04:36.789 --> 04:41.690
Dr. Barbara Hagan, obviously overqualified.

04:44.231 --> 04:51.253
Just tried everything to make sure he knew the more analytical side of English and it's not just the curtains were blue which symbolizes sadness.

04:51.893 --> 04:56.514
Yeah, but she was unfortunately ran out of the woodlands.

04:57.035 --> 05:01.496
She was teaching lenses of English and one of them is a Marxist lens.

05:01.516 --> 05:05.977
She was not saying she's a huge Marxist, but there is a kid who is part of the Turnpoint USA club.

05:06.552 --> 05:11.793
He brought that to parents and she was alleviated of her duties.

05:12.013 --> 05:12.553
That's wild.

05:12.573 --> 05:14.114
I thought that's still happening.

05:14.234 --> 05:14.974
Absolutely.

05:15.014 --> 05:19.915
That was 2023.

05:19.955 --> 05:24.616
2023 and it's still people, the Marxist is scary.

05:25.476 --> 05:31.357
It's the most frustrating because she left academia because she said it focused too much on research and she just wanted to teach.

05:32.387 --> 05:33.107
That's so rare.

05:33.547 --> 05:34.347
That's so rare.

05:34.427 --> 05:39.969
And she could have gone to a private or a small liberal arts school, but she chose to credit English.

05:40.729 --> 05:41.389
Yeah.

05:41.669 --> 05:42.249
Yeah.

05:42.809 --> 05:53.092
I really credit them too for, you know, like if I do defend successfully my PhD, like I would definitely make sure to knock on wood to find them and be like, you are a huge part of this.

05:53.532 --> 05:54.052
That's great to hear.

05:54.092 --> 05:54.432
I love it.

05:54.492 --> 05:56.233
I love seeing where it comes from.

05:56.313 --> 05:56.773
Oh yeah.

05:57.193 --> 05:57.833
No, totally.

05:57.873 --> 05:58.173
And like,

05:58.735 --> 06:01.117
So I particularly focus on Native American history.

06:01.578 --> 06:07.343
And I was torn between that or Holocaust studies, two very lighthearted areas of history.

06:08.343 --> 06:12.147
But growing up, my parents' favorite movie, one of theirs, is Dancing with Wolves.

06:13.008 --> 06:14.589
And obviously it's not historically the best.

06:15.129 --> 06:18.192
But it was just always kind of in the background.

06:18.532 --> 06:25.919
My parents are really big history fans, maybe not in the academic sense, but more just in popular culture or documentary.

06:26.560 --> 06:35.204
My mom's a huge reader, so it's always been encouraged to, you know, listen to the real quote-unquote real story or give space for them.

06:35.364 --> 06:36.925
Right, absolutely.

06:37.285 --> 06:38.545
Can I ask if your parents are educated?

06:38.726 --> 06:39.686
Yeah.

06:39.746 --> 06:41.487
My father went to Miami University.

06:41.787 --> 06:44.008
It is in Oxford, Ohio.

06:44.148 --> 06:44.828
I almost went there.

06:44.848 --> 06:47.930
I was accepted, but it was $30,000 for out of state.

06:47.950 --> 06:48.450
Thanks.

06:52.405 --> 06:58.532
But yeah, my dad, he majored in sports management, but now he is the VP of an all-in-one company for supply chain.

06:58.652 --> 07:03.879
So just because you get a degree in something doesn't mean that's what you have to do.

07:03.979 --> 07:07.984
And then my mom went to Ohio State for a semester before she went to cosmetology school.

07:08.084 --> 07:09.866
So me being A&M,

07:10.296 --> 07:12.678
graduating, very, very big deal.

07:12.758 --> 07:18.723
My mom does not take this for granted and really instilled that in me and Rachel, my twin sister, to try to do something.

07:18.803 --> 07:20.204
I was about to ask about your siblings too.

07:20.224 --> 07:25.328
Yeah, that's the one thing that you really can't get taken away from you is that education and the skills you learn in the classroom.

07:25.748 --> 07:28.771
That's very true.

07:28.811 --> 07:34.015
So come from a very academic family or even seems like a hardworking type of family.

07:34.935 --> 07:38.458
So did you ever feel a lot of pressure or have that type of...

07:40.377 --> 07:44.320
No, I never felt like if I didn't bring home an A, I'd be grounded for a month.

07:44.380 --> 07:46.802
It was more of just, don't be a dumbass.

07:46.862 --> 07:48.023
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

07:48.324 --> 07:49.164
It's kind of expected.

07:49.224 --> 07:50.365
It's just, you excel.

07:50.625 --> 07:53.828
You know, I was never pressured to have an actual job in high school.

07:54.208 --> 07:55.209
School was the job.

07:55.950 --> 08:00.193
So it was just trying to never to quit on anything that you make a commitment.

08:00.213 --> 08:01.054
You've got to do it.

08:01.114 --> 08:03.136
That was as well as theater and improv.

08:04.016 --> 08:05.978
Just give 110% to everything you do.

08:07.239 --> 08:09.081
My mom's dad farmed for 40 years, so...

08:11.025 --> 08:12.506
A hard worker.

08:12.526 --> 08:13.627
Yeah.

08:13.647 --> 08:16.048
Do you think that those traits have followed you through?

08:17.790 --> 08:19.671
How do you see that in your...

08:22.933 --> 08:25.315
I think just not taking it for granted.

08:25.555 --> 08:31.919
I think sometimes you see kids on campus who college to them was never a question.

08:33.260 --> 08:40.165
It's just something they have to do to check off the box and maybe they're not appreciating of like, this might be the one time in my life

08:41.131 --> 08:46.516
where I just get to read something and talk about it and try to expand yourself as a human, as a scholar.

08:46.536 --> 08:48.818
I think that's so, so, so special.

08:49.278 --> 09:00.368
And I think, you know, it's maybe the first time in a lot of society's history where a majority of its populace are educated, literate, and trying to improve in that area.

09:00.608 --> 09:01.229
Very good point.

09:01.269 --> 09:01.409
Um...

09:03.584 --> 09:11.469
So we talked a little bit about this, about where history came from, but what made you choose Native American history?

09:11.509 --> 09:12.469
Yeah, I can tell you exactly.

09:12.489 --> 09:17.792
So I was taking Native American history, a little survey course with Dr. Angela Hudson.

09:18.713 --> 09:22.635
I was in M207, this building over here on campus.

09:22.855 --> 09:24.076
It's the A in engineering.

09:24.376 --> 09:26.097
For some reason, a lot of history seminars are there.

09:26.117 --> 09:28.739
It's like a huge, you know, 400-person seminar room.

09:28.779 --> 09:29.959
Oh, okay.

09:30.660 --> 09:31.040
And...

09:32.092 --> 09:35.295
I just remember sitting there one day and I'm like, this is what I want to do.

09:36.275 --> 09:40.438
Um, obviously it's not a, I'm an outsider.

09:40.579 --> 09:44.442
I am not indigenous culturally related.

09:44.702 --> 09:47.944
I'm not enrolled in any federally recognized tribes or state recognized tribes.

09:48.705 --> 09:48.865
Um,

09:49.562 --> 09:55.905
But it makes you view America in maybe a different light, more honest, more truthful.

09:56.205 --> 10:01.207
It's a harder light to look at it, but it makes you feel more connected to the land, its people, its cultures.

10:01.228 --> 10:09.471
Yeah, and it's not typically the first thing, or maybe in bold when you read textbooks.

10:09.511 --> 10:13.373
Maybe it's a little sigh, and then once you get to a trail of tears, it's never really brought up again.

10:13.433 --> 10:17.635
And that's something I'm very passionate about, is bringing it into the main historical conversation.

10:19.927 --> 10:20.507
Yeah, I mean.

10:21.028 --> 10:21.528
That makes sense.

10:21.708 --> 10:23.749
Yeah, and Dr. Hudson is now serving as my advisor.

10:23.769 --> 10:25.110
Full circle.

10:25.130 --> 10:25.930
It's full circle.

10:26.030 --> 10:30.913
It was just a once-in-a-lifetime opportunity, really, really, really.

10:31.273 --> 10:34.795
How did you, was the first time you met her was in that class?

10:34.815 --> 10:35.056
Yes.

10:35.256 --> 10:35.536
Okay.

10:35.576 --> 10:46.182
Yes, but it's pretty, it's a bigger class, so I don't know if we ever got a lot on one-on-one time, and then that following semester, or so fall, no, spring of 23, yeah, spring of 23,

10:47.279 --> 10:52.503
She was teaching her smaller 300-level class called Native Americans of the South.

10:53.584 --> 10:54.425
And I took that as well.

10:55.566 --> 10:57.707
And again, just completely fall in love with it.

10:57.807 --> 11:00.610
I would consider myself a Southern historian.

11:01.390 --> 11:03.312
And I actually met a future roommate there.

11:03.332 --> 11:04.873
That's so cool.

11:04.913 --> 11:07.715
Yeah, just has overflowed my cup in so many ways.

11:07.815 --> 11:12.018
But that's when we definitely had a way closer intimate working relationship.

11:12.559 --> 11:13.880
And then that summer...

11:14.693 --> 11:15.994
I was graduating early in August.

11:16.014 --> 11:19.577
So I'm class of 25, but graduated May.

11:19.677 --> 11:20.818
No, August the 24th.

11:21.178 --> 11:22.299
So I'm a full year old.

11:23.020 --> 11:26.943
And I basically reached out to her and said, like, hey, I need a job.

11:27.063 --> 11:28.964
Like, do you need someone to refill coffee?

11:29.044 --> 11:31.046
Like, anything, anything, anything I'll do for you.

11:31.506 --> 11:33.488
And she said, like, I was actually about to reach out to you.

11:34.128 --> 11:36.010
I need a research assistant for a project.

11:36.850 --> 11:42.535
And that's what kind of got me on board to do actual factual history and not just reading about it.

11:43.093 --> 11:47.076
I was researching a group of Native Americans who were here between 1904 and 1911.

11:47.096 --> 11:50.958
They were football players here before we officially integrated.

11:51.679 --> 11:53.600
And it's, you know, transnational.

11:53.640 --> 11:55.121
We're dealing with people from Oklahoma.

11:56.162 --> 11:57.543
We have a Cheyenne individual.

11:57.563 --> 12:00.565
They're from Carlisle, the boarding school in Carlisle, Pennsylvania.

12:01.305 --> 12:10.351
And, yeah, it just completely transforms what you think of A&M when we realize who was actually here taking classes before maybe we were in that national.

12:10.691 --> 12:11.272
Right, yeah.

12:12.888 --> 12:14.089
Intention Spotlight?

12:14.109 --> 12:15.069
Yeah.

12:15.350 --> 12:17.691
So we worked on that research together for a year.

12:17.751 --> 12:22.154
We presented a paper together at the Alliance for Texas History Conference in May of 25.

12:22.174 --> 12:25.336
That was my first conference.

12:25.556 --> 12:30.499
And then I basically told her, like, hey, I'm applying for a PhD.

12:30.539 --> 12:32.440
Like, I really want to do this for a while.

12:32.460 --> 12:34.361
I think this fills me.

12:34.441 --> 12:35.402
Like, this is amazing.

12:35.422 --> 12:37.243
So here we are.

12:37.603 --> 12:39.565
I am finished with my first semester as of

12:40.593 --> 12:41.514
Fall of 25.

12:42.034 --> 12:43.355
And now we're entering spring 26.

12:43.555 --> 12:44.215
Congratulations.

12:44.235 --> 12:44.555
That's awesome.

12:44.575 --> 12:45.276
Thank you.

12:45.296 --> 12:50.638
Actually, can you go back and talk a little bit about the Native Americans that were here before?

12:50.658 --> 12:50.759
Yeah.

12:50.779 --> 12:52.740
Because I have no knowledge of this.

12:52.760 --> 12:53.720
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

12:53.740 --> 12:56.482
So we found six in this time frame.

12:56.922 --> 12:59.023
All of them but one are Choptop.

12:59.403 --> 12:59.683
Okay.

12:59.943 --> 13:01.544
Which we think there might be something there.

13:02.545 --> 13:06.267
And the other one that I mentioned, Cheyenne, his name is Michael Valenti.

13:07.187 --> 13:10.029
He is actually from someone who survived a wounded knee.

13:10.842 --> 13:13.043
His mother survived him, didn't he?

13:13.503 --> 13:15.164
And he actually went on to marry.

13:16.084 --> 13:17.445
She's Alaskan native.

13:17.505 --> 13:22.547
Her name is Cecilia Baranovich, and her dad was actually a Russian trapper mogul.

13:22.987 --> 13:25.788
So, insane.

13:25.908 --> 13:29.469
And Michael went on after being here at A&M.

13:29.889 --> 13:31.370
He coached for Baylor.

13:32.050 --> 13:37.072
He had an amazing career in the minor league baseball as well as professionals.

13:37.112 --> 13:39.453
He was part of the Cincinnati Reds for a little bit, so that was...

13:40.053 --> 13:42.515
As a Cincinnati native, that was fun.

13:42.595 --> 13:45.177
And then we also have George Marlowe.

13:46.398 --> 13:47.800
He was also Choctaw.

13:48.200 --> 13:58.529
Oh gosh, we have Sig Flincham, James Flincham, nicknamed Sig, who we think is married during his time here, which is odd because where was his wedding staying?

13:58.609 --> 13:58.929
Right.

13:59.469 --> 14:07.356
So unfortunately, a lot more questions are not answered with these men because there's a lot of questions about if they're being paid or not.

14:08.257 --> 14:08.417
Um...

14:10.096 --> 14:15.978
Because we have records of Michael Valenti and Victor Kelly called Chalk as he's Choctaw.

14:16.319 --> 14:18.119
Chalk Kelly being paid at Carlisle.

14:18.900 --> 14:26.082
So it's kind of hard to imagine why they would leave there and come to Texas when they have absolutely no ties here.

14:27.143 --> 14:27.943
What year is this?

14:28.003 --> 14:28.883
I'm sorry.

14:28.983 --> 14:32.605
It's between about 1904 to 1911.

14:32.765 --> 14:38.947
And this is a football period in which we have the head coach of Charles Moran

14:39.696 --> 15:04.098
uncle charlie okay yeah yeah yeah that's yes so this is the only other time where a and m stopped playing ut there was claims that he had professionals which might have been those carlisle native players if they're being paid um and basically they said we're not playing you until moran's gone and we didn't play them for three years that's wild um so yeah we have

15:04.695 --> 15:06.456
A lot of evidence of them being involved in clubs.

15:06.476 --> 15:06.996
Mm-hmm.

15:07.016 --> 15:19.381
They were very active in Ross Volunteers, which is ironic, as Sol Ross is a proclaimed, quote-unquote, Indian killer, yet he has indigenous people probably honoring him and his memory and his impact at Texas A&M.

15:21.162 --> 15:22.223
They are part of the Glee Club.

15:23.643 --> 15:25.784
They are known as, quote, ladies' men.

15:26.284 --> 15:26.484
Oh, okay.

15:26.524 --> 15:27.965
With these white women in Bryan.

15:28.005 --> 15:28.165
Mm-hmm.

15:28.205 --> 15:31.747
So it raises questions of interracial relationships.

15:32.027 --> 15:32.307
Right.

15:32.893 --> 15:33.893
So yeah, it's very exciting.

15:33.913 --> 15:35.974
It's probably what's going to be my master's thesis.

15:35.994 --> 15:36.654
Yeah.

15:37.034 --> 15:37.454
So yeah.

15:37.514 --> 15:38.535
Thank you for sharing that.

15:38.755 --> 15:45.297
That was like just a person for me personally, because I'm trying to put different pieces of this puzzle together from an outsider.

15:45.317 --> 15:45.557
Yeah.

15:46.077 --> 15:49.018
There's so many pieces that just don't make sense yet.

15:49.278 --> 15:49.498
No.

15:49.558 --> 15:59.361
And so there was a fire in 1912 that like did away with the old main building, which has all of our records of where they slept, which I need to know.

15:59.401 --> 15:59.801
Yeah.

15:59.861 --> 16:02.582
Just a lot of those records that unfortunately are lost that would

16:02.918 --> 16:04.279
probably answer a lot of those questions.

16:05.320 --> 16:06.480
Do you want me to...

16:06.701 --> 16:12.064
I have over 2,000 oral histories from Aggies, their wives.

16:12.244 --> 16:17.988
Do you want me to go through those and see what I... Also, you can have access to them.

16:18.108 --> 16:19.489
I'll make sure you get that.

16:20.430 --> 16:21.230
Because this...

16:22.111 --> 16:24.993
I feel like a lot of this is brought up in some of them.

16:25.473 --> 16:32.638
Yes, so Chuck Kelly and Mike Valenti are probably the two biggest successes in terms of their football career while they're here.

16:33.263 --> 16:36.604
Um, they were nominated for like a Southwest all time.

16:36.724 --> 16:38.144
Who would be the best team?

16:38.284 --> 16:40.205
It's both of them.

16:40.845 --> 16:44.366
Um, Richard Kelly went on to live in LA, um, after.

16:45.026 --> 16:49.768
Um, he was a PE coach for like 50 years at Hollywood High School.

16:49.808 --> 16:50.348
That's wild.

16:50.488 --> 16:54.869
And then he actually was a technical advisor for one of Judy Garland's first films, Pigskin Parade.

16:55.209 --> 16:58.210
Um, Texas State accidentally gets picked to play Yale.

16:58.230 --> 16:58.290
Um,

16:58.818 --> 17:02.741
in the championship, so they have to rally the troop, rally the men.

17:03.682 --> 17:06.164
Yeah, I actually got in contact with his granddaughter, Linda.

17:06.324 --> 17:07.365
So that was an amazing phone call.

17:07.585 --> 17:08.246
That's awesome.

17:08.266 --> 17:08.646
Yeah.

17:09.287 --> 17:09.867
Yeah.

17:09.967 --> 17:11.488
No, it really has changed.

17:11.588 --> 17:18.554
You know, sometimes I think with the Southern schools, you think a very specific group of people were sitting at that table.

17:18.734 --> 17:18.914
Right.

17:18.954 --> 17:22.237
And I think constantly looking at, is that true?

17:22.718 --> 17:23.038
Right.

17:23.238 --> 17:25.220
We have records of women taking classes here.

17:25.580 --> 17:25.900
Before.

17:26.240 --> 17:27.842
Before the 20th century.

17:27.922 --> 17:28.102
Yep.

17:29.506 --> 17:31.127
And as well as Mexican-Americans.

17:31.247 --> 17:31.767
Absolutely.

17:31.807 --> 17:32.307
Mexican-Americans.

17:32.967 --> 17:33.868
So many.

17:33.888 --> 17:35.208
So many Central Americans.

17:35.248 --> 17:35.748
So many.

17:36.048 --> 17:36.589
So many.

17:36.629 --> 17:39.590
We have so many ags from India.

17:39.610 --> 17:40.670
Mm-hmm.

17:41.190 --> 17:43.271
I mean, it's crazy, crazy, crazy.

17:43.311 --> 17:45.111
And they're probably in these yearbooks.

17:45.612 --> 17:52.094
Because the most interesting thing about my native ags, which I forgot to mention, never graduated until Bill Leftwich in 1954.

17:53.394 --> 17:53.554
Odd.

17:54.155 --> 17:54.715
So they're here.

17:54.735 --> 17:55.215
Mm-hmm.

17:59.778 --> 18:00.239
Interesting.

18:00.520 --> 18:01.061
I wonder what that is.

18:01.081 --> 18:03.226
I think that might have been there only taking a class or two.

18:04.208 --> 18:04.548
Oh.

18:04.589 --> 18:07.876
But then, this is at a time when the whole school is working on the core schedule.

18:09.052 --> 18:32.975
are you doing all day what are you doing all right yeah because it's it's very strict very strict very strict yeah i mean there's in the yearbook of like what time breakfast is what time march is what time is everyone walking the fourth period like it's a very hell military school it 100 is so we just want to know did that cause animosity or could it not because dr kelly was awarded most popular cadet for superlatives in one of the yearbooks

18:33.926 --> 18:37.269
Um, so these are very well-known people on campus.

18:37.549 --> 18:37.889
Yeah.

18:38.090 --> 18:41.192
So it is fascinating that there is no sign of them today.

18:41.372 --> 18:42.113
Yeah, that's so interesting.

18:42.133 --> 18:47.857
When arguably the beginnings of A&M football being commercially successful is this team.

18:48.738 --> 18:48.998
Hmm.

18:49.859 --> 18:50.640
That's a big mystery.

18:50.960 --> 18:51.801
Yes, it is.

18:51.861 --> 18:53.682
I see why you're interested in that.

18:53.822 --> 18:54.823
I'm a football nut.

18:54.963 --> 18:55.644
I really am.

18:56.004 --> 19:01.989
I, obviously, the amount of money the school's invested into it, I think that's partly why I'm like, okay, well, if

19:03.196 --> 19:28.415
there's some areas of the school that are not as funded but we're putting hundreds of million dollars into our coaching salaries and nil and our facilities i'm gonna i'm gonna check in um obviously there's a lot of literature on native sport obviously with jim thorpe and um you know carlisle football team completely transformed the game as we know it so

19:29.326 --> 19:32.967
They're very, very interconnected.

19:33.187 --> 19:34.467
Another side thing.

19:35.888 --> 19:44.770
I think we just, not inherited, but we're now housing all of the old film reels from the football.

19:45.310 --> 19:45.750
Do you?

19:46.330 --> 19:46.530
Yep.

19:46.670 --> 19:46.971
Okay.

19:47.031 --> 19:47.291
Okay.

19:47.311 --> 19:47.891
Just making sure.

19:48.411 --> 19:50.411
Oh my God.

19:50.732 --> 19:51.112
Oh.

19:51.132 --> 19:52.072
Because there's photos.

19:52.092 --> 19:54.312
We have photos of them in these games sometimes.

19:54.432 --> 19:56.093
And it's like just being able to pick them out.

19:56.273 --> 19:56.513
Right.

19:56.823 --> 20:03.305
Because only Victor Kelly, I'd say, is phenotypically someone you could appoint as indigenous.

20:03.825 --> 20:04.646
So there's something to that.

20:05.086 --> 20:05.626
Very much so.

20:05.706 --> 20:08.467
And they're calling him Choctaw.

20:08.927 --> 20:10.948
They are not hiding their nativeness.

20:11.668 --> 20:15.509
In the yearbook, they hail, oh, he's from the new state of Oklahoma.

20:16.049 --> 20:17.190
He does the corn dance.

20:17.390 --> 20:18.410
He does the war whoops.

20:18.890 --> 20:19.610
But it's fascinating.

20:19.650 --> 20:23.352
The more they become integrated into campus, the less they comment on their nativeness.

20:23.972 --> 20:24.532
That's interesting.

20:24.592 --> 20:24.772
Uh-huh.

20:25.773 --> 20:36.096
So we kind of question, like, is this Aggie, the binding force, is this able to trump racial lines?

20:36.136 --> 20:37.016
That's strong, yeah.

20:37.036 --> 20:37.877
Yeah, there's something to that.

20:37.957 --> 20:38.657
It truly is.

20:41.658 --> 20:45.139
This is also going to be a random side thing, but you brought up the Aggie spirit.

20:46.139 --> 20:50.461
And I've heard so many different people say what it means to them.

20:50.841 --> 20:51.881
What does that mean to you?

20:54.740 --> 20:59.603
I would say, really and truly, just taking the harder path, if it's the right path.

21:01.204 --> 21:03.326
I would say that is probably the Aggie way.

21:03.506 --> 21:06.028
Like, why am I getting choked up?

21:06.048 --> 21:08.009
My parents were at the airport the other day.

21:08.049 --> 21:10.511
They didn't go to A&M, but they were wearing A&M merch.

21:11.091 --> 21:14.653
And they saw a young mom, just alone, just kind of struggling.

21:14.674 --> 21:18.116
We had a toddler crying and, like, another kind of pulling on our arm.

21:18.136 --> 21:19.557
And she's obviously having a hard time.

21:19.577 --> 21:21.858
And my mom just walks up to her and says, like, can I hold?

21:21.878 --> 21:23.419
Do you need to go to the bathroom or something?

21:23.439 --> 21:24.180
We can watch them.

21:24.728 --> 21:26.629
And she said, like, we call you all our Aggies.

21:27.329 --> 21:27.930
Of course.

21:29.090 --> 21:36.634
So, you know, I know people make fun of it, a cult or whatever, but you can go anywhere, anywhere, anywhere, and you will find someone.

21:36.934 --> 21:42.477
And that is just enough to share a meal or lend a helping hand.

21:42.497 --> 21:44.478
And I think that is so, so, so important today.

21:44.598 --> 21:45.478
That's so powerful.

21:45.799 --> 21:46.199
Truly.

21:46.219 --> 21:46.419
Yeah.

21:47.873 --> 21:50.855
And I hear all these stories about people giving their senior rings.

21:50.955 --> 21:51.275
Oh, yeah.

21:51.335 --> 21:56.679
And then they see somebody else out in the wild with it, and they just instantly have that connection.

21:56.719 --> 21:57.219
Yeah, yeah.

21:57.780 --> 22:02.163
That's so fascinating to me because I've never really – this all is very new.

22:02.183 --> 22:02.543
Oh, absolutely.

22:02.623 --> 22:15.632
And so, like, coming in from the outside, that's just like – It is a whole new world, and part of it breaks my heart because I want to say out of, like, 12 of my cohort for grad school, maybe only two of us went to undergrad here.

22:16.132 --> 22:16.373
Oh.

22:16.613 --> 22:16.813
Mm-hmm.

22:17.765 --> 22:19.727
So they don't love it like we do.

22:20.047 --> 22:21.508
It doesn't maybe mean as much to them.

22:23.230 --> 22:29.816
You know, they came here specifically for a professor or maybe the stipend was the most handsome or something like that.

22:31.157 --> 22:36.221
So they didn't get to really experience all the charm that A&M has to offer.

22:37.883 --> 22:43.047
So yeah, I do try to, you know, amp it up or say, like, you really should go to a football game.

22:43.087 --> 22:45.129
When you're here, baseball's super fun.

22:46.484 --> 22:48.545
From baseball, it's easier to get in and out.

22:48.565 --> 22:52.506
And, you know, our women volleyball team just brought home our first national title.

22:52.626 --> 22:54.046
And it's just very exciting.

22:54.086 --> 22:55.246
Basketball's doing good.

22:55.466 --> 22:57.787
I have no idea what that would look like.

22:57.827 --> 22:58.107
Yeah.

22:58.347 --> 23:01.508
Yeah, I mean, it's just a very, very exciting time.

23:02.548 --> 23:03.909
Athletic.

23:04.369 --> 23:05.429
Athletically.

23:05.509 --> 23:06.149
Athletically.

23:06.169 --> 23:07.110
Yeah, athletically.

23:08.788 --> 23:16.112
So obviously you were interested in history and as an undergrad you came here, but what made you continue on as that?

23:16.312 --> 23:17.013
Dr. Hudson.

23:17.833 --> 23:18.353
Specifically?

23:18.414 --> 23:19.654
Yes, Dr. Hudson.

23:20.795 --> 23:25.397
Native studies is kind of a smaller area.

23:26.098 --> 23:29.320
My husband's also getting his master's at A&M in engineering.

23:29.760 --> 23:33.522
I joke A&M owns us, but we just bought a house here.

23:33.542 --> 23:35.783
We're putting roots down.

23:35.903 --> 23:37.985
We're guaranteed here for five years with my program.

23:39.254 --> 23:40.315
It just makes the most sense.

23:40.335 --> 23:43.577
He also works full-time, so I did not apply to anywhere else.

23:44.238 --> 23:44.638
This is it?

23:44.798 --> 23:47.920
Yes, and I only applied out of Lone Star Community College today.

23:48.541 --> 23:48.961
Wow.

23:50.022 --> 23:50.602
That's impressive.

23:51.223 --> 23:52.484
People say don't do that.

23:52.564 --> 23:57.287
A lot of people say do not do that for undergrad and graduate, but I knew.

23:57.687 --> 23:58.468
If you love it, you love it.

23:58.488 --> 23:59.128
I love it.

23:59.829 --> 24:03.692
Yeah, I'll be an ag for, oh gosh, undergrad was three.

24:04.752 --> 24:05.373
Yeah, I was eight years.

24:08.401 --> 24:10.062
You're almost an old egg now.

24:10.242 --> 24:11.842
Almost old egg.

24:12.022 --> 24:14.103
Almost old egg.

24:14.163 --> 24:22.705
It's funny to me that the Aggies that I've talked to, they didn't go here, but they came as a professor or something.

24:22.745 --> 24:26.506
They feel like they can't claim being an Aggie.

24:26.786 --> 24:28.767
And I'm like, you taught here for 25 years.

24:29.027 --> 24:29.767
It makes me really sad.

24:29.787 --> 24:32.388
It makes me sad because you're shaping these Aggies.

24:33.308 --> 24:33.908
They're learning things.

24:34.717 --> 24:41.481
what that Aggie spirit work ethic looks like through you and the rigor that you are demanding from them.

24:41.621 --> 24:43.221
That's 110% part of it.

24:43.381 --> 24:43.842
Absolutely.

24:44.122 --> 24:45.523
Yeah, that upsets me.

24:45.743 --> 24:50.005
Yeah, it's one of those things in the back of my head that makes me really sad that I hear.

24:50.025 --> 25:01.551
No, I mean, yeah, I didn't go to Miami or Ohio State, but, you know, you feel that connection through family or through just being associated with it for time.

25:01.571 --> 25:02.311
That makes sense.

25:03.032 --> 25:05.493
I have no problem if you teach your routine.

25:05.713 --> 25:06.193
Right, right.

25:06.214 --> 25:06.414
Yeah.

25:06.774 --> 25:10.055
Or, like, even I encourage my kids to get their ad hearing.

25:10.535 --> 25:11.056
Yeah.

25:11.476 --> 25:12.256
Yeah, exactly.

25:12.376 --> 25:12.717
I know.

25:12.797 --> 25:13.657
It's expensive.

25:13.677 --> 25:14.377
You put in the work.

25:14.397 --> 25:15.278
You put in the work.

25:15.378 --> 25:18.159
And it's a wonderful tradition that not every school honors.

25:18.179 --> 25:20.000
I mean, we have one of the highest graduation rates.

25:21.038 --> 25:21.699
Oh, I didn't know that.

25:21.859 --> 25:22.419
Absolutely.

25:22.679 --> 25:23.280
Okay, that makes sense.

25:23.300 --> 25:23.900
Yes, absolutely.

25:24.080 --> 25:25.401
I want to say we're in the 90s.

25:26.302 --> 25:26.802
That's unheard of.

25:26.922 --> 25:27.843
It's not 90s, 80s.

25:27.943 --> 25:28.704
Yeah, that's unheard of.

25:28.724 --> 25:29.424
That's unheard of.

25:29.745 --> 25:30.305
Absolutely.

25:30.965 --> 25:33.888
Like, I don't think I've literally heard of any other university like that.

25:33.908 --> 25:36.449
No, because it is, it's a part.

25:36.590 --> 25:40.773
If you say to someone, I went to A&M, and you don't see their ring, you ask, well, is it getting resized?

25:41.153 --> 25:41.693
What's wrong?

25:41.753 --> 25:42.994
Is there something wrong?

25:43.074 --> 25:43.655
It's a part of it.

25:43.695 --> 25:44.455
It's 110% part of it.

25:44.535 --> 25:46.217
I mean, I have a paperweight.

25:47.343 --> 25:57.988
Like, when Will, my husband, got his ironing, my brother got his ironing, and me, my mom, there's this Etsy cellar that makes, like, a wooden ornament of it with your ear.

25:58.348 --> 25:59.489
Oh, that's really sweet.

25:59.709 --> 26:01.049
Yeah, like, 100%.

26:01.309 --> 26:01.710
Very much so.

26:01.750 --> 26:01.950
Yeah.

26:03.310 --> 26:05.691
So, your brother, what was his field?

26:06.051 --> 26:06.652
Parks and Rec.

26:07.292 --> 26:07.592
Wow.

26:07.692 --> 26:07.932
Yeah.

26:08.192 --> 26:08.593
Awesome.

26:08.653 --> 26:09.713
We need people like this.

26:09.773 --> 26:13.515
Yes, we put the and in A and M. We are not A or M. Yes.

26:20.417 --> 26:26.715
park so he actually um just accepted a new job we're very excited for him he is an aquatics man

26:27.343 --> 26:32.365
So he runs and pretty much makes sure that lifeguards are being trained properly.

26:32.385 --> 26:33.226
The equipment is good.

26:33.266 --> 26:34.166
Patrons are happy.

26:34.206 --> 26:35.146
Pools are clean.

26:35.607 --> 26:36.647
It's running smoothly.

26:36.667 --> 26:37.367
That's what he does.

26:37.668 --> 26:41.089
He did that for the Woodlands Township for 10 years.

26:41.109 --> 26:43.290
He was there from 16 to Trumpet.

26:43.910 --> 26:45.671
And now he's moved to San Antonio for that.

26:45.691 --> 26:49.213
So he's doing 23 facilities, one year-round auditorium.

26:49.293 --> 26:50.313
We could not be more excited for him.

26:50.473 --> 26:50.933
That's awesome.

26:50.953 --> 26:52.214
That's amazing.

26:52.234 --> 26:53.815
So...

26:56.864 --> 26:58.785
Dr. Hudson, what...

26:58.805 --> 27:01.927
Okay, she also is Native American Studies as well, right?

27:01.947 --> 27:02.127
Yes.

27:02.827 --> 27:03.087
Okay.

27:03.127 --> 27:04.568
So what is her teaching style?

27:04.588 --> 27:07.109
Because I've never taken a class here, obviously, but... Yeah.

27:07.629 --> 27:21.896
So I would definitely say she is trying to get you prepared for asking questions that you just have to approach every single text with, every single primary document with, and every single archive trip.

27:22.256 --> 27:23.277
What am I asking?

27:23.317 --> 27:23.777
Who wrote it?

27:23.797 --> 27:24.578
Where do they go to school?

27:24.598 --> 27:25.338
Who's their advisor?

27:27.094 --> 27:27.894
It's all connected.

27:27.974 --> 27:30.415
She really stresses that.

27:30.615 --> 27:32.235
You know, what sources are they engaging in?

27:32.735 --> 27:33.835
What secondary sources?

27:33.875 --> 27:35.096
Who's she pushing back on?

27:35.196 --> 27:38.136
Whose work are they building upon or who are they kind of disputing?

27:40.177 --> 27:49.899
You know, when you go into an archive, it can be simple as take a picture of what you want and then write a little note to yourself because you're going to forget in two weeks why you took a photo of that.

27:50.159 --> 27:54.800
She taught me try to be kind to your future forgetful self.

27:55.562 --> 27:58.783
She says that sometimes, and I always try to remember that.

28:00.483 --> 28:01.683
She's very primary-based.

28:01.703 --> 28:03.424
That's so important.

28:03.464 --> 28:10.085
In our 300-level class, she'd have a worksheet of, like, okay, pull out a line, explain the source they got this from.

28:10.205 --> 28:12.406
Is this a good interpretation of the source?

28:13.346 --> 28:17.047
You don't see that a lot in history classes at, like, those levels.

28:17.107 --> 28:25.249
It's mainly, again, I think it's just a misconception of the history field in general of just regurgitating a timeline or something that we completely diverge from.

28:25.748 --> 28:28.470
past 100 level class, especially here at A&M.

28:29.230 --> 28:37.376
But yeah, she's a wonderful, wonderful person to learn how to dissect, look at that document in the context, how does that impact.

28:37.816 --> 28:40.078
She also comes with an English background.

28:40.658 --> 28:42.199
She has a master's in English as well.

28:42.219 --> 28:42.279
So,

28:45.451 --> 28:46.612
Reading.

28:46.672 --> 28:47.312
Reading.

28:47.332 --> 28:50.874
Yeah, and that's what a lot of my personal statement was for the grad program.

28:51.014 --> 28:52.455
I have a huge reading background.

28:52.535 --> 28:54.196
I am a huge reader.

28:54.316 --> 28:56.137
I read to work in over at the English department.

28:56.157 --> 28:58.998
It was incredibly impactful to me.

28:59.639 --> 29:01.920
Just meeting the source where it's at.

29:02.400 --> 29:02.981
Right, yeah.

29:03.001 --> 29:05.302
A hundred percent.

29:05.662 --> 29:08.223
When you were undergrad, you said you were English, correct?

29:08.524 --> 29:12.186
What professors there, what kind of skills were transferable to?

29:12.426 --> 29:14.647
Yeah, so I...

29:15.456 --> 29:23.419
It was an English minor, so I took, you know, the comp and rec at the dual credit level in high school.

29:23.820 --> 29:36.325
So then when I got here, I took, oh gosh, a 19th century American novel with Ira Dworkin, who's fabulous, who wrote a rec letter for me.

29:36.545 --> 29:40.867
I took Chaucer with Dr. Britt Mize, all in Middle English.

29:41.007 --> 29:42.788
I read The Canterbury Tales, all in Middle English.

29:42.808 --> 29:43.628
That was fantastic.

29:44.565 --> 29:51.067
And then with Dr. Cooper, I took American novel World War II on.

29:52.128 --> 30:00.711
And as far as Dr. Workman goes, again, just it's really easy to bring the 21st century with you when you're looking at stuff.

30:00.731 --> 30:02.031
When you're reading Uncle Tom's Cabin.

30:02.591 --> 30:02.891
Yeah.

30:02.911 --> 30:07.153
When you're reading, you know, Squatter and the Dawn or something like that.

30:08.440 --> 30:14.985
When you're reading Mark Twain, you want to make it accessible to you, but you just can't do it all the time.

30:15.146 --> 30:18.388
And he would assign a healthy portion every night.

30:18.488 --> 30:25.654
You'd come to class reading 100 pages each time, and that does prep you for grad school, but also just how can I read efficiently and effectively?

30:26.674 --> 30:33.540
And then Brett Mize, he was wonderful, and the very first day of class, it's a 400-level Charleston class.

30:34.741 --> 30:34.981
And...

30:35.666 --> 30:37.287
he said something Middle English.

30:37.307 --> 30:38.828
He goes to saying, this is how it would sound.

30:39.288 --> 30:41.990
And I swear to you, I heard two kids drop right then and there.

30:42.110 --> 30:44.032
Like, I cannot do this.

30:44.172 --> 30:45.052
Right.

30:45.993 --> 30:47.294
Oh my gosh.

30:47.314 --> 30:49.275
I really, really stuck with me.

30:49.375 --> 30:50.536
Just aspects of Chaucer.

30:50.556 --> 30:52.758
You know, Pinterberry Towels.

30:53.518 --> 31:00.183
It's literally a tale-telling contest on a very serious pilgrimage.

31:00.463 --> 31:05.186
So it's just between, it's a balance between earnest and game.

31:06.306 --> 31:26.688
okay and that is where I really rest how I approach a grad seminar when I'm answering questions because I think academia is very very stuffy and unacceptable and it is this wall and it doesn't have to be so I try to always manage the earnestness and gain and whatever I'm doing um and then that last English class I took

31:27.568 --> 31:30.590
There's a wonderful book called Sex and the Cherry by Jeanette Winterson.

31:30.650 --> 31:33.411
It's a post-modernist work, and it's just absurd, and it's awesome.

31:33.571 --> 31:35.272
And I highly recommend it.

31:35.332 --> 31:35.912
It's just good.

31:36.012 --> 31:41.735
It's just good stuff, and there's no one universal truth, all that good stuff, post-nihilism.

31:41.795 --> 31:42.936
You can get it from World War II.

31:44.182 --> 31:55.255
Um, so how do you actually, um, on one hand, people say history is very one-way, linear, and then on the other hand, there is no one truth.

31:55.295 --> 31:55.495
Yes.

31:55.595 --> 31:56.797
How do you marry those two?

31:56.817 --> 31:57.438
I'm allowed.

31:57.938 --> 32:00.822
I am no one truth, especially if you're an indigenous person.

32:01.542 --> 32:26.982
scholar like indigenous studies scholar like myself or if you focus on black history or Mexican-American history or any minority or marginalized history you are not the spotlight so that dominant narrative you can go but all day and I think you have to accept that frustration that you have to meet that middle and it's maybe the more uncomfortable version of

32:27.733 --> 32:34.924
But trying to be respectful to both sides and not just painting one as the villain and one as the savior, that doesn't do anything good.

32:35.144 --> 32:35.344
No.

32:35.464 --> 32:36.646
