We're not saying you're wrong, you're a horrible person, you're a burning hell.

01:06:05.791 --> 01:06:06.832
No one's saying that.

01:06:08.793 --> 01:06:12.255
Difficult conversations are wonderful to have and you will come out stronger together.

01:06:12.636 --> 01:06:13.596
I promise you.

01:06:13.616 --> 01:06:15.217
That's very good.

01:06:15.357 --> 01:06:16.218
That's a very good point.

01:06:16.238 --> 01:06:16.958
Thank you.

01:06:16.998 --> 01:06:17.619
It's a very good point.

01:06:18.935 --> 01:06:19.135
It's...

01:06:24.158 --> 01:06:25.579
I think parents right now are just...

01:06:26.279 --> 01:06:31.822
There's anxieties in the country as a whole, and they're just... What is the closest thing that resembles that right now?

01:06:31.902 --> 01:06:35.224
What is something that I can mirror, put that on?

01:06:35.244 --> 01:06:39.066
And I think that right now is the conversation that's closest to home for parents.

01:06:39.267 --> 01:06:40.047
I think that's all that is.

01:06:40.527 --> 01:06:40.827
That's right.

01:06:41.668 --> 01:06:41.908
Yeah.

01:06:43.009 --> 01:06:44.510
I'm not a parent, so... Me neither.

01:06:44.530 --> 01:06:46.291
Like, I can't...

01:06:46.551 --> 01:06:47.211
But...

01:06:49.487 --> 01:07:05.298
I think that's something that no matter what side you're on, that you really need to, even if things make you uncomfortable, as you said, to engage with these things and to understand that being uncomfortable, we're not attacking, no one's attacking you.

01:07:05.378 --> 01:07:06.198
No one's attacking you.

01:07:06.859 --> 01:07:15.465
You know, students, if you are feeling that, you know, if your parents ask you what you're reading and you know they're not going to like something, talk it through with them.

01:07:15.805 --> 01:07:15.965
Mm-hmm.

01:07:16.635 --> 01:07:19.197
Point to things that you did like, things that you didn't like.

01:07:19.277 --> 01:07:22.320
If you say, I completely disagreed with that entire book, move on.

01:07:22.480 --> 01:07:25.162
That's 100% okay.

01:07:25.923 --> 01:07:28.284
I've read books and I'm like, the argument didn't sell me.

01:07:28.485 --> 01:07:28.925
That's okay.

01:07:30.827 --> 01:07:32.488
It can really be as simple as that.

01:07:33.509 --> 01:07:34.349
It really can be.

01:07:34.550 --> 01:07:39.293
It doesn't have to be, I have to email the person who wrote this, demand, they lose their job.

01:07:39.353 --> 01:07:40.995
Like, no, it's not that deep.

01:07:41.395 --> 01:07:42.356
It's pretty good.

01:07:43.477 --> 01:07:44.177
I think also that

01:07:45.886 --> 01:08:15.466
people are equating what professors are teaching with what they personally believe and those two things aren't the same no like it kind of it frustrates me that this actually became the word and the language that university implemented when doing the syllabus reviews but I on the current record I said like educating someone is not advocating so then I kind of bit my tongue when that became the slogan of saying are you advocating

01:08:15.813 --> 01:08:16.713
For these ideologies.

01:08:18.655 --> 01:08:19.335
That's so vague.

01:08:19.515 --> 01:08:20.656
That's so vague.

01:08:20.676 --> 01:08:21.876
And they did that on purpose.

01:08:21.956 --> 01:08:26.959
It's easier to just brush everything under a rug and throw it away and light it on fire.

01:08:26.979 --> 01:08:28.980
That's 100% easier.

01:08:29.800 --> 01:08:31.401
How do you know if it's advocating?

01:08:32.582 --> 01:08:32.762
Right.

01:08:33.142 --> 01:08:35.043
Are you wearing a pride flag?

01:08:35.103 --> 01:08:37.044
Like, what does that look like?

01:08:37.545 --> 01:08:37.825
Yeah.

01:08:38.165 --> 01:08:43.628
It's just this vague monster that they can constantly change.

01:08:43.648 --> 01:08:44.348
The oogie boogie.

01:08:44.528 --> 01:08:45.189
The oogie boogie.

01:08:45.309 --> 01:08:45.529
Yeah.

01:08:46.599 --> 01:08:46.799
Yeah.

01:08:48.480 --> 01:08:50.700
So that's the scariness of it.

01:08:50.860 --> 01:08:54.241
It can constantly change what they are attacking.

01:08:55.221 --> 01:08:58.222
And I think that's what every department needs to be scared of.

01:08:58.682 --> 01:09:00.843
Every single department needs to be scared of.

01:09:00.943 --> 01:09:01.483
Every department.

01:09:02.043 --> 01:09:04.384
Okay, if one department's on the chopping block, everybody is.

01:09:04.404 --> 01:09:04.644
Yes.

01:09:05.864 --> 01:09:06.024
Yes.

01:09:06.404 --> 01:09:07.645
And I'm not saying complete gut.

01:09:07.665 --> 01:09:08.345
Yeah.

01:09:08.805 --> 01:09:10.025
I'm saying professors.

01:09:10.305 --> 01:09:10.525
Right.

01:09:11.706 --> 01:09:13.006
Your citizenship status.

01:09:14.286 --> 01:09:15.907
If you're married to the same sex.

01:09:17.784 --> 01:09:20.105
Because they are saying one thing with this.

01:09:20.805 --> 01:09:22.106
The public is personal.

01:09:22.706 --> 01:09:23.666
And the personal is public.

01:09:24.287 --> 01:09:25.867
That's 110% what it is.

01:09:26.487 --> 01:09:28.108
So, it's just McCarthyism.

01:09:28.308 --> 01:09:29.208
It's neo-McCarthyism.

01:09:29.228 --> 01:09:29.809
That's all it is.

01:09:30.849 --> 01:09:31.589
That's all it is.

01:09:31.889 --> 01:09:34.991
I've literally been saying that.

01:09:35.011 --> 01:09:36.171
McCarthyism never went away.

01:09:36.191 --> 01:09:38.852
It just changed.

01:09:41.973 --> 01:09:44.874
I'm glad that I got to talk to you.

01:09:44.995 --> 01:09:45.995
It's good seeing...

01:09:46.973 --> 01:10:16.384
that okay this is going to sound really bad but it's good to see that we're we're not the only ones that are scared absolutely all of us are yeah i'd be more concerned if you pulled a kid over like you know you're waiting for the bus the shuttles and they have no idea what's going on that's concerning that's way worse in my opinion than being like i'm 110 for this i'd rather you be 110 for this and having no idea what's going on because that means you're actually reading informed yeah being informed yep

01:10:18.622 --> 01:10:23.303
And that's one of the things that this university is supposed to do is make it informed citizens.

01:10:23.523 --> 01:10:23.683
Yep.

01:10:24.184 --> 01:10:25.404
That's part of the land grant mission.

01:10:25.684 --> 01:10:26.084
Exactly.

01:10:26.464 --> 01:10:27.544
Informed citizens.

01:10:32.826 --> 01:10:41.748
So, um, not that I, I, it is, it, it is very like we're both, we're same hat.

01:10:41.768 --> 01:10:47.630
We both have, we're both, it's, it's, it's comforting and also very sad that that's happening.

01:10:48.070 --> 01:10:48.270
Um,

01:10:48.948 --> 01:10:52.850
And this directly, obviously, affects you because you're teaching your TA.

01:10:52.870 --> 01:10:54.010
Yes.

01:10:54.811 --> 01:10:59.413
So how did you, whenever you got that news, what was the things that were going through your head?

01:11:00.654 --> 01:11:02.975
I was heartbroken.

01:11:03.535 --> 01:11:04.015
I still am.

01:11:04.936 --> 01:11:05.656
I am heartbroken.

01:11:06.756 --> 01:11:14.200
I did a guest lecture in the last semester about the 60s and 70s, and I was going to do actual social history on it.

01:11:14.500 --> 01:11:16.361
I had slides I was making.

01:11:17.315 --> 01:11:20.176
And I had a photo of Marsha P. Johnson, and I thought, I can't.

01:11:20.796 --> 01:11:21.116
I can't.

01:11:22.677 --> 01:11:23.017
I can't.

01:11:23.237 --> 01:11:28.459
So I just did pop culture in the 60s and 70s, and I slipped some stuff in.

01:11:28.719 --> 01:11:30.540
I was like, hey, look, the Beatles have long hair now.

01:11:31.520 --> 01:11:34.841
But then Mia Farrow, Marsbury's baby, has short hair.

01:11:35.642 --> 01:11:36.802
What's that?

01:11:37.102 --> 01:11:41.964
So I tried to make it accessible in ways that they can see concretely.

01:11:42.204 --> 01:11:43.584
I think that was how I really tried.

01:11:43.704 --> 01:11:45.205
I tried to do that, and...

01:11:47.189 --> 01:12:03.940
I think another thing I strongly believe is that people who are against teaching about gender don't realize that the fact of even in their binary, to teach a binary, you're still teaching gender.

01:12:04.540 --> 01:12:06.421
You can't teach about anything.

01:12:13.352 --> 01:12:13.532
Yeah.

01:12:14.432 --> 01:12:16.673
You can't teach about heteronormative marriage.

01:12:16.893 --> 01:12:17.193
Nothing.

01:12:18.413 --> 01:12:20.054
And that, I can't mention I'm married to a man.

01:12:22.995 --> 01:12:23.535
That's upsetting.

01:12:23.735 --> 01:12:25.215
That's...

01:12:25.235 --> 01:12:30.277
The Aggie that I met here on campus that is a master's engineering student.

01:12:31.117 --> 01:12:32.437
An exemplary Aggie.

01:12:32.597 --> 01:12:33.478
I can't mention that.

01:12:33.498 --> 01:12:36.778
You can't talk about your... That's because that's part of you.

01:12:36.838 --> 01:12:37.599
That's part of me.

01:12:38.339 --> 01:12:38.619
So...

01:12:40.686 --> 01:12:43.288
That feeling of having to leave parts of you behind.

01:12:43.308 --> 01:12:44.309
Parts of you behind.

01:12:44.509 --> 01:12:50.794
And if you want to get technical, okay, you're telling a university that didn't let me accept women until 66.

01:12:50.814 --> 01:12:53.956
They're getting rid of women's studies.

01:12:56.498 --> 01:12:58.500
Even though A&M is mostly women today.

01:12:59.221 --> 01:13:00.341
Oh, I didn't know that either.

01:13:00.762 --> 01:13:02.583
There's more women in college than men right now.

01:13:03.544 --> 01:13:06.126
I think that's scaring some people.

01:13:06.486 --> 01:13:07.107
I would say yes.

01:13:07.567 --> 01:13:08.308
Yeah.

01:13:08.328 --> 01:13:08.488
Yeah.

01:13:13.553 --> 01:13:19.917
How much time do you generally would put into creating those lessons?

01:13:20.358 --> 01:13:21.038
Oh my gosh.

01:13:22.099 --> 01:13:22.879
Three hours at least.

01:13:24.400 --> 01:13:24.821
That's a lot.

01:13:25.541 --> 01:13:28.603
Because it's making slides, making sure they're accessible slides.

01:13:28.843 --> 01:13:30.484
It's double-checking my sources.

01:13:31.905 --> 01:13:32.866
It's so much.

01:13:32.926 --> 01:13:33.707
And that's per lesson.

01:13:34.087 --> 01:13:34.827
Yeah, that's per lesson.

01:13:34.847 --> 01:13:36.288
And I only did that one guest lecture.

01:13:36.769 --> 01:13:38.330
But, you know, they encourage...

01:13:39.050 --> 01:14:04.850
new profs to pretty much like over the summer write all your lectures so you can just hit the ground running and not have to play catch up and you can do an honest job on it and not just freak out right and I leaned on other grad students and said hey what do you have during this time period that I should probably mention too um yeah yeah yeah so to see that

01:14:05.455 --> 01:14:09.136
You are encouraged to make it before, and you put so much time into it.

01:14:09.896 --> 01:14:12.217
So to receive those emails of... To delete it.

01:14:12.757 --> 01:14:13.217
Exactly.

01:14:14.338 --> 01:14:14.918
Slap in the face.

01:14:15.258 --> 01:14:16.178
It's a slap in the face.

01:14:20.779 --> 01:14:21.219
I'm sorry.

01:14:21.399 --> 01:14:23.420
No, I mean, just everyone has a gender.

01:14:24.360 --> 01:14:25.961
Everyone has sexual orientation.

01:14:26.061 --> 01:14:27.081
Everyone has a race.

01:14:27.961 --> 01:14:31.242
Everyone has an ethnic, racial belonging.

01:14:31.902 --> 01:14:33.443
These are all things you fail on the SAT.

01:14:35.281 --> 01:14:35.461
Right.

01:14:37.082 --> 01:14:37.843
It's okay.

01:14:39.203 --> 01:14:40.604
It doesn't have to separate.

01:14:40.624 --> 01:14:43.006
That's the whole point of why we're talking about it.

01:14:43.026 --> 01:14:45.307
So we can have a more cohesive and new bio today.

01:14:46.187 --> 01:14:47.788
So I think that's the most embarrassing thing.

01:14:48.429 --> 01:14:53.672
And I think the line they were saying is they didn't want white kids to feel guilty.

01:14:53.992 --> 01:14:55.773
That's, yeah, that was brought up a lot.

01:14:56.213 --> 01:15:03.838
Unless your last name is Lee and you're related to Robert E. Mm-hmm.

01:15:04.903 --> 01:15:05.704
How would you know?

01:15:06.424 --> 01:15:09.427
Unless your mom's a united daughter of confederacy.

01:15:09.867 --> 01:15:10.568
Also even then.

01:15:10.588 --> 01:15:11.969
Also even then.

01:15:12.369 --> 01:15:15.151
It's one of those things of, like, the sins of our fathers.

01:15:15.252 --> 01:15:19.215
And if you're so far gone, I don't think you're feeling bad sitting in class.

01:15:19.855 --> 01:15:20.496
That's my thing.

01:15:22.117 --> 01:15:28.022
If you are so on that spectrum, I don't think you're feeling bad.

01:15:28.042 --> 01:15:28.242
No.

01:15:28.883 --> 01:15:30.964
You're looking at that through nostalgia.

01:15:31.004 --> 01:15:31.665
You're not.

01:15:32.265 --> 01:15:32.786
No, I think...

01:15:33.276 --> 01:15:33.976
That pisses me off.

01:15:33.996 --> 01:15:35.457
And I'm not just saying that as a northerner.

01:15:36.057 --> 01:15:36.877
That pisses me off.

01:15:36.997 --> 01:15:39.318
Like, what do you mean you feel guilty?

01:15:39.338 --> 01:15:40.679
Yeah.

01:15:41.319 --> 01:15:41.879
You didn't do it.

01:15:42.179 --> 01:15:43.000
You didn't do it.

01:15:43.060 --> 01:15:48.262
You should be disgusted in your country and try to have a better tomorrow that actually fulfills the Constitution.

01:15:48.282 --> 01:15:48.422
Mm-hmm.

01:15:50.246 --> 01:15:56.608
That's why I kind of brought up those hard, like, how would you address those hard conversations and those uncomfortable.

01:15:56.788 --> 01:15:57.228
Yeah.

01:15:57.448 --> 01:16:04.490
Because I think people also equate having those hard conversations where they're uncomfortable with needing to feel guilty.

01:16:04.530 --> 01:16:05.630
Those things don't need to happen.

01:16:05.650 --> 01:16:07.210
Those things don't need to happen.

01:16:07.911 --> 01:16:16.513
You can feel guilty as an American of I'm in the South that was built on the economy of this chattel slavery empire that we were.

01:16:17.984 --> 01:16:19.504
How are we addressing that today?

01:16:19.524 --> 01:16:21.445
It does not have to be shameful.

01:16:21.465 --> 01:16:22.385
It does not have to be with guilt.

01:16:23.185 --> 01:16:29.087
If we're approaching it with honesty, honestly trying to make the tomorrow better, I think that's beautiful and wonderful.

01:16:29.727 --> 01:16:33.868
So that whole child guilt thing, how do you measure that?

01:16:35.269 --> 01:16:36.609
Hey, raise your hand if you feel guilty.

01:16:37.729 --> 01:16:38.790
Okay, still 1 to 10.

01:16:40.010 --> 01:16:40.330
Yeah.

01:16:40.874 --> 01:16:41.774
How do you actually do that?

01:16:42.395 --> 01:16:45.256
Again, it's this elusiveness that means nothing.

01:16:45.276 --> 01:16:46.677
Don't give them an idea.

01:16:46.937 --> 01:16:50.539
It's this elusiveness that you cannot completely implement in the classroom.

01:16:50.639 --> 01:16:50.919
Yeah.

01:16:51.259 --> 01:16:51.399
Oh.

01:16:52.020 --> 01:16:56.762
Yeah, that's not something like, it's not on the end of year or end of semester surveys.

01:16:56.882 --> 01:16:57.302
No.

01:16:57.803 --> 01:16:58.123
No.

01:16:58.183 --> 01:16:58.543
Yeah.

01:16:59.163 --> 01:16:59.424
No.

01:17:03.546 --> 01:17:05.767
Like I said, the uncomfortable means you're human.

01:17:06.807 --> 01:17:08.068
I'm glad you're feeling something.

01:17:08.408 --> 01:17:11.371
If you're not feeling anything, learning about these horrific events.

01:17:11.391 --> 01:17:11.911
That'd be weird.

01:17:12.552 --> 01:17:13.613
You're weird.

01:17:13.793 --> 01:17:14.854
Like, that's weird.

01:17:14.894 --> 01:17:16.595
It truly would be weird if you didn't.

01:17:16.675 --> 01:17:17.016
Yeah.

01:17:17.136 --> 01:17:18.817
So, it's my two cents.

01:17:18.877 --> 01:17:19.158
Yeah.

01:17:19.818 --> 01:17:20.038
Yeah.

01:17:20.739 --> 01:17:28.206
I think it's valuable because as a historian, you see that what's happened and the patterns.

01:17:29.027 --> 01:17:29.867
Yeah.

01:17:29.887 --> 01:17:30.748
The patterns are scary right now.

01:17:30.808 --> 01:17:31.429
Yeah, you're right.

01:17:31.489 --> 01:17:31.589
Yeah.

01:17:32.401 --> 01:17:34.583
Because that's the first day of every history class.

01:17:34.643 --> 01:17:36.125
It's like, why do we study history?

01:17:36.465 --> 01:17:39.829
And without fail, a kid will raise their hand and say, so we don't repeat it.

01:17:41.811 --> 01:17:43.193
And you just want to roll your eyes.

01:17:43.934 --> 01:17:44.674
History doesn't repeat.

01:17:44.714 --> 01:17:45.355
It rhymes.

01:17:46.376 --> 01:17:47.998
It's not like an exact copy.

01:17:48.318 --> 01:17:48.519
Right.

01:17:49.199 --> 01:17:51.502
So, but you know, happy that he rose his hand.

01:17:54.423 --> 01:17:55.224
Hey, that's something.

01:17:55.484 --> 01:17:56.224
Something, yeah.

01:17:56.244 --> 01:17:56.664
Something.

01:17:57.485 --> 01:17:59.246
So, going to something a little lighter.

01:17:59.506 --> 01:18:00.267
Yeah.

01:18:00.607 --> 01:18:06.090
What is your favorite part about teaching and about even just studying history?

01:18:06.110 --> 01:18:07.311
What's your favorite parts about it?

01:18:07.872 --> 01:18:10.433
I was surprised by how much I enjoyed my guest lecture.

01:18:11.414 --> 01:18:17.298
I improvised some of it, and, you know, getting back to my theatrical roots is always fulfilling.

01:18:18.618 --> 01:18:19.299
Just seeing...

01:18:20.163 --> 01:18:25.227
The spark, the connection, the faces change when they're understanding something.

01:18:25.267 --> 01:18:25.927
That's priceless.

01:18:26.528 --> 01:18:27.849
That is utterly priceless.

01:18:29.430 --> 01:18:32.612
And they walk out and go like, that was my favorite lecture.

01:18:33.753 --> 01:18:36.755
That's so special.

01:18:37.876 --> 01:18:41.959
In terms of just loving history, I think just because it's everywhere.

01:18:41.979 --> 01:18:45.782
If you're more connected, your country, your people...

01:18:47.929 --> 01:18:56.414
You feel less alone, I think, because you know that people have been feeling similarly since human existence.

01:18:56.894 --> 01:19:04.978
Like when you're reading Chaucer and there's a line about jealousy or sadness or rage, I'm okay because someone in 1394 felt that.

01:19:05.239 --> 01:19:06.499
So I'm going to get through this.

01:19:07.560 --> 01:19:11.262
So yeah, it's just the human condition I think is very, very comforting.

01:19:11.669 --> 01:19:34.357
it truly is that's what i was like yeah yeah yeah even through time yeah we can find comfort in that we find comfort in that the people have always been people truly and like that way is yeah also amazing way but love is just everywhere really and truly without being really corny no it yeah love is really really everywhere and that that's probably the best part that's really touching

01:19:36.100 --> 01:19:42.303
Um, so did you want to be a lecturer or do you want to continue to be a researcher?

01:19:42.323 --> 01:19:52.248
What's the... That's something I've been grappling with that I hope I can answer more concretely probably by the time I'm ending PhD.

01:19:52.428 --> 01:19:57.471
I think it was easy to do that pop culture lecture because like the stakes were lower.

01:19:58.171 --> 01:20:04.154
Me talking about best picture winners versus having to explain, you know, Indian removal.

01:20:05.439 --> 01:20:08.320
I feel a lot of weight in the ladder.

01:20:08.380 --> 01:20:16.204
That feels very, very heavy responsibility-wise because I acknowledge completely that might be the only time people hear about it.

01:20:17.405 --> 01:20:17.665
Yeah.

01:20:17.905 --> 01:20:18.545
Not watch it.

01:20:18.585 --> 01:20:18.825
Right.

01:20:19.005 --> 01:20:20.706
In, you know, Dances of the Wolves.

01:20:20.826 --> 01:20:21.046
Right.

01:20:21.106 --> 01:20:22.467
But truly learns about it.

01:20:24.288 --> 01:20:25.288
So that's very daunting.

01:20:25.768 --> 01:20:30.931
Obviously, research is fun, you know, getting unique,

01:20:31.939 --> 01:20:50.832
answers and filling in gaps where people may have never thought about and getting a fuller picture that's always wonderful um but yeah I really really enjoy teaching so we'll see yeah yeah but I think A&M does a wonderful job even though it isn't R1 it encourages actual education

01:20:53.424 --> 01:20:55.125
It's not just someone reading off slides.

01:20:55.145 --> 01:20:57.507
They encourage that connection, I think.

